From 2263155432a28064db1e48540d4cf8ecc4c3a5e6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 19:55:45 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] EDITION ONLY: restore the views of an anamorphic 3D video
 from the stored samples

When a stereoscopic 3D stream has pixels that are not square (its sample aspect ratio is
not 1:1: a half side by side video with a doubled ratio, say, as FFmpeg gives it for
Matroska's StereoMode and for x264's frame packing with a VUI ratio), the FFmpeg backend
scaled the whole packed frame to the shape of its pixels with swscale's bicubic filter
before anything else saw it, and the consumer took the views out of that picture. The
filter chosen for the views (VideoOutput::stereoScaling) never applied to the larger
part of the restoration.

For a frame with views side by side or on top of each other the backend now leaves the
frame at the size it is stored in and says the ratio in the new
QVideoFrameFormat::pixelAspectRatio(); the packing is the one of the frame's own
side data, or the stream's last (the frame packing SEI comes with keyframes only).
VideoOutput shows the frame at the shape of its pixels as before (the item's size, the
shape rule of stereoShapeFallback) and restores each view from the stored samples to the
size of a view with its stereoScaling filter, in the layer and for a single eye. The
stereoView Flat picture of such a frame is no longer scaled by swscale but by the scene
graph's texture sampling. 2D frames, and 3D frames of the other packings, go the path
Qt has always had.

Edition only: the backend cannot know whether the consumer will take the views apart, so
for the default stereoView of the offerable part (Flat, Qt's own picture) it changes how
a 3D video with a sample aspect ratio is scaled, and the new format field is API.

Assisted-by: Claude Sonnet via Claude Code
